<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_teaserfigure"><img src="2509.11062v1/teaser.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/173;" width="476" height="173" alt="The left panel depicts user frustration with manual slide authoring, while the right panel highlights user satisfaction after receiving AI-generated slides.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An overview of <span id="S0.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>’ capabilities.</span> <em id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left:</em> Users can generate complete academic presentation slides from an academic paper. <em id="S0.F1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right:</em> Users can iteratively revise the generated slides by providing high-level natural language instructions, enabling efficient and precise slide editing.<span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">The left panel depicts user frustration with manual slide authoring, while the right panel highlights user satisfaction after receiving AI-generated slides.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) has opened new opportunities for education. While learners can interact with academic papers through LLM-powered dialogue, limitations still exist: absence of structured organization and high text reliance can impede systematic understanding and engagement with complex concepts. To address these challenges, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>, an LLM-driven system that converts research papers into pedagogically structured, multimodal slides (<em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, diagrams and tables). Drawing on cognitive science, it creates a presentation-oriented narrative and allows iterative refinement via an interactive editor, in order to match learners’ knowledge level and goals. <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> further incorporates verification and knowledge retrieval mechanisms to ensure accuracy and contextual completeness. Through extensive user studies, <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> enhances learners’ comprehension and engagement compared to conventional LLM-based reading. Our contributions lie in designing a multi-agent framework for transforming academic papers into pedagogically optimized slides and introducing interactive customization for personalized learning.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Computing education, Human-computer interaction, Information visualization
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has significantly transformed the landscape of natural language processing and artificial intelligence. Models such as GPT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, PaLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhery et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and LLaMA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding, generating, and reasoning with human language, enabling a wide range of applications in information retrieval, automated writing, programming assistance, and beyond. With their growing accessibility through public APIs and interactive interfaces, LLMs are increasingly integrated into both professional and educational contexts, reshaping how individuals acquire, process, and apply information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alongside these technological developments, the integration of LLMs into education and learning has emerged as a notable trend. Unlike traditional learning methods, where students passively consume materials such as textbooks or academic papers, LLM-based systems allow learners to interact directly with content in a dynamic and dialogic manner. For example, instead of merely reading an academic paper, a learner can upload the document to an LLM-powered system and engage in iterative questioning and clarification. Through this interactive process, learners are able to query definitions, verify interpretations, and explore related concepts in real time. This paradigm shift holds the potential to make complex academic knowledge more approachable, supporting personalized exploration, fostering deeper comprehension, and reducing barriers to engaging with highly specialized content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, directly learning from LLM-based interactions still presents notable limitations. First, the knowledge acquired through dialogue often lacks structure. Academic papers, for example, are carefully organized into sections that convey background, motivation, methodology, experiments, and conclusions in a coherent framework. When learners interact with LLMs through isolated questions and answers, they primarily focus on specific details while losing sight of the overall structure of the work. This piecemeal approach makes it difficult to form a systematic understanding of the material. Second, the output modality of current LLM-based learning is predominantly textual. While text-based explanations are useful, they fall short in domains where comprehension is enhanced by multimodal representations, such as diagrams, tables, and visual summaries. The absence of such elements can hinder learners’ ability to grasp complex ideas and relationships.
These limitations highlight the need for systems that can leverage the capabilities of LLMs to present knowledge from academic materials in a structured, learner-friendly, and multimodal format. In particular, we focus on the task of learning from research papers, where both systematic organization and visual representation play critical roles. To address this need, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>, an LLM-driven system that automatically transforms research papers into visually rich, well-structured, and pedagogically optimized slide decks, thereby facilitating more effective and engaging learning experiences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Transforming a research paper into effective presentation slides poses several challenges. First, there is a fundamental gap between the narrative style of academic writing and the pedagogical logic required for teaching and learning. Research papers are typically written to document novelty and rigor, often assuming that readers already possess relevant background knowledge. This structure, while suitable for scholarly communication, can be difficult for learners to follow and may obscure the key ideas. Educational psychology provides evidence that careful reorganization of content can significantly enhance learning effectiveness. For example, Cognitive Load Theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sweller, 1988</a>)</cite> suggests that instructional materials should be arranged to reduce extraneous cognitive load, enabling learners to focus their mental resources on essential understanding. Mayer’s Multimedia Learning Theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer, 2009</a>)</cite> further emphasizes that presenting information in a coherent sequence, and combining verbal and visual forms, facilitates comprehension and knowledge retention. Drawing on these principles, we design a content reorganization agent that converts the logical flow of a paper into a presentation-oriented narrative. Specifically, we adopt a Problem–Method–Result–Conclusion (PMRC) framework to restructure the background, motivation, methodology, and findings of a paper into a format that is easier to follow, thereby helping learners better comprehend and internalize the material.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Second, effective learning requires adaptability to diverse learner backgrounds and goals. A static set of slides may not be sufficient, as learners differ in their prior knowledge, interests, and the level of detail they require. To overcome this limitation, we introduce an interactive editor agent that supports iterative refinement of the automatically generated slides. Through natural language dialogue, users can request modifications—for example, adding additional slides on specific concepts or simplifying technical details. This interactive capability enables the system to dynamically tailor slide decks to individual learners, ensuring that the generated materials are not only accurate and coherent but also aligned with the user’s unique learning needs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, beyond the content reorganization and interactive editing capabilities introduced above, <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> incorporates two additional mechanisms to further enhance the reliability and educational value of the generated slides. First, a Verification–Adjustment Loop compares the slide content against the source paper to identify and repair omissions or inaccuracies, ensuring complete and faithful coverage of key contributions. Second, an external knowledge retrieval module augments the Editor Agent, enabling it to enrich slides with background information from cited or related works when users request clarification of unfamiliar concepts. Guided by overarching principles from educational psychology and cognitive science, these complementary components strengthen <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>’s ability to deliver accurate, comprehensive, and contextually enriched learning materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct multiple user studies to evaluate <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> from different perspectives, including the overall quality of the generated slides, the usefulness of the interactive customization function, and the comparative advantages of using <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> versus directly reading papers with LLM assistance. The results consistently demonstrate that <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> facilitates deeper understanding and better retention of complex academic content.
In summary, this paper makes the following key contributions: 1) We design a novel system that automatically transforms academic papers into structured, visually enriched, and pedagogically informed slide decks, drawing on principles from educational psychology and cognitive science. 2) We introduce an editor agent that supports dynamic and dialogic refinement of slides, allowing learners to personalize content according to their knowledge background, learning goals, and preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs for Content Generation</span> Our work is built upon the capabilities of LLMs, a class of pre-trained models that have demonstrated significant performance improvements on a wide range of Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks. The foundation of these models is the Transformer architecture, which utilizes self-attention mechanisms to process text in a highly parallelizable manner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. LLMs are pre-trained on large-scale, unlabeled text corpora using self-supervised objectives like language modeling, where the goal is to predict the next token in a sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. This pre-training phase allows the model to acquire a broad base of world knowledge and linguistic patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key finding in the development of LLMs is the principle of scaling laws, which empirically show that model performance improves predictably as model size, dataset size, and computational resources increase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. When these models exceed a certain scale, they begin to exhibit "emergent abilities" not present in smaller models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. One of the most notable emergent abilities is in-context learning (ICL), where a model can perform a new task by conditioning on just a few examples provided in a prompt, without any updates to its parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Another critical advancement is the ability to align LLM behavior with human intent and values through techniques like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), which fine-tunes models based on human preferences to make them more helpful and harmless <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These core capabilities in language understanding, generation, and instruction following are fundamental to our agent’s task of transforming the content of an academic paper into a structured presentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based Autonomous Agents</span> While LLMs provide foundational language capabilities, organizing them into agentic systems enables them to address more complex, multi-step problems autonomously. An agent is a system that can perceive its environment, make decisions, and take actions to achieve a goal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wooldridge and Jennings, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. LLM-based agents use an LLM as their core reasoning engine or "brain" to orchestrate this process. Research has shown that these agents can be designed with several key components, including memory to retain information over time, planning capabilities to decompose complex goals into smaller sub-tasks, and the ability to use external tools to acquire new information or perform specific actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The application of LLM-based agents has shown promising results in a variety of domains that require collaboration and specialized roles. For instance, in software development, multi-agent systems can simulate a team of engineers with roles like coder, product manager, and tester to autonomously develop software <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Another prominent application is the simulation of human-like social behaviors in sandbox environments, where agents with distinct personas interact with each other to produce emergent social dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These systems often employ specific communication structures, such as layered or centralized networks, and paradigms like cooperation or debate to coordinate agent actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Our paper-to-slide system is conceptualized as such a collaborative agent system, where the complex task of presentation generation is broken down into specialized sub-tasks like content extraction, summarization, and structural organization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human-Computer Interaction with Intelligent Agents</span> The successful deployment of LLM-powered systems depends on effective human-computer interaction (HCI). A significant challenge for users, including experts, is the "blank page problem," which describes the difficulty of formulating effective prompts or workflows to evaluate and interact with LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Arawjo, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zamfirescu-Pereira et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This highlights the need for interfaces and systems that can better guide users and translate their high-level goals into concrete actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A central theme in HCI research for AI is alignment, which involves ensuring that an AI system’s actions and outcomes align with the user’s values, goals, and expectations. Recent work has expanded this concept to include process and specification alignment, emphasizing that users need control over not just the final output but also the intermediate steps the AI takes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terry et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This requires designing for mixed-initiative interaction, where both the user and the agent can guide the workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horvitz, 1999</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, guidelines for human-AI interaction recommend that systems should make their capabilities and limitations clear, support efficient invocation and dismissal, and allow for easy correction of errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amershi et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Our system is designed with these principles in mind, positioning the agent as an assistant that generates an editable first draft of a presentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. This allows the user to maintain control, verify the generated content, and refine the final output to meet their specific rhetorical and aesthetic goals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11062v1/section/img/method.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/139;" width="476" height="139" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of <span id="S3.F2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>.</span> Our multi-agent architecture orchestrates the transformation of academic papers into presentation slides through a three-phase pipeline: (1) Content Understanding and Structuring, where the Parser and Planner Agents analyze the source material and design the slide structure in JSON format specifying content, figures, and tables for each slide. (2) Quality Assurance and Refinement, where the Verification and Adjustment Agents ensure content fidelity and completeness. (3) Generation and Interactive Optimization, where the Generator Agent produces the final presentation slides in LaTeX code format and the Editor Agent facilitates human-in-the-loop revisions via natural language dialogue.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the methodology underlying <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>, a multi-agent system designed to transform academic papers into structured presentation slides. The system breaks down the slide production process into multiple agents, each assigned distinct responsibilities and working together within a coordinated framework. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the system architecture integrates three primary modules: the <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parser Agent</span> and <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planner Agent</span> that analyze source material and define the presentation structure; the <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification–Adjustment Agents</span> that ensure accuracy, consistency, and completeness; and the <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generator Agent</span> and <span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Editor Agent</span> that produce and refine multi-modal content with human-in-the-loop optimization. These modules form a coordinated pipeline that delivers slides meeting standards of accuracy, clarity, and usability.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Content Understanding and Structuring</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This initial phase tackles the dual challenges of accurately extracting multi-modal information from structurally complex PDFs and re-conceptualizing the narrative for a presentation format.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">High-Fidelity Multi-modal Parsing.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental challenge in automating slide generation is the robust parsing of academic PDFs, which contain a rich mixture of text, figures, tables, and complex mathematical expressions. Simple text extraction methods often fail to preserve crucial layout and semantic relationships. To overcome this, our Parser Agent employs a two-stage strategy. Initially, it utilizes the Marker<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DataLab, 2024</a>)</cite> model to perform a high-fidelity conversion of the PDF into a structured Markdown format, preserving the document’s overall layout and identifying graphical elements. To further enhance the extracted information for subsequent processing, the agent performs an explicit extraction of tables and mathematical expressions using a LLM. The input to this stage is the complete Markdown representation of the paper produced in the last stage, which preserves both text content and the original Markdown encoding of tables and equations. The LLM is instructed to identify all table blocks and equation blocks from this Markdown text, and for each block, to preserve its original syntax without modification. These extracted blocks are then stored as separate entries in the slide-planning JSON file (see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS7" title="A.7. Enhanced Content JSON Example ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>), alongside their original positions in the paper. By isolating tables and equations in this way, the downstream agents can access them directly without scanning the full document, which reduces the risk of context truncation in long inputs and prevents structural errors in numerical data or mathematical notation during later processing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cognitive-Driven Presentation Planning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Academic papers typically follow the IMRaD structure<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Day and Gastel, 2012</a>)</cite> (Introduction, Methods, Results, and Discussion), which is effective for reading but inefficient for quick information delivery in oral presentations. To address this, the Planner Agent takes the structured content extracted by the Parser Agent and reorganizes it into a PMRC (Problem–Motivation–Results–Conclusion) presentation flow using LLMs. First, it identifies the communicative role of each section (<em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, methodology, key results, conclusions) through LLM-based semantic classification, and ranks them by their importance to the overall narrative. Guided by Cognitive Load Theory (CLT)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sweller, 1988</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sweller et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, extraneous cognitive load is reduced by restricting each planned slide to a single central message, removing tangential or repetitive details, and sequencing content so that conceptual complexity increases incrementally. Next, applying Mayer’s Multimedia Learning Principles<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer, 2009</a>)</cite>, the agent enriches each core message with the most relevant figure selected from those extracted by the Parser Agent: for this, the complete original captions are provided to the LLM, which uses semantic similarity to match figures to the slide’s message. The LLM then rewrites each matched caption into a concise form to fit the slide layout while retaining key information, and positions text and visual elements for simultaneous viewing to support integrated verbal–visual processing. Finally, for each slide, the LLM generates concise bullet points and draft speaker notes that align with the planned message and selected visual aids. The complete plan is output as a JSON file specifying slide sequence, text content, associated figures (with shortened captions) and tables, and preliminary notes, ready for direct use by the Generator Agent to produce <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> slides.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Quality Assurance and Content Adjustment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although LLM-based planning effectively structures slide content, LLMs have inherent limitations such as context length constraints, where earlier information may be omitted, and hallucination tendencies, where generated content can deviate from facts in the source paper. In automated slide generation, these issues risk omitting important findings or introducing inaccuracies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we introduce a two-stage quality assurance step involving a Verification Agent followed by an Adjustment Agent when needed. The Verification Agent calls an LLM to compare the generated slide plan against the structured content extracted from the original paper, focusing on methodology, results, and conclusions. Instead of requiring exact matches, it uses a loose semantic criterion in which mentioning the core concept counts as coverage. It then assesses each content area for coverage sufficiency; only when overall coverage is clearly insufficient or high-importance items are missing does it flag the plan for revision. In that case, the Adjustment Agent is triggered to repair only the high-importance omissions. This involves locating the relevant material in the original paper, integrating it into existing slides where possible, or creating a new supplemental slide if necessary. The LLM reformulates the retrieved content into 2–4 concise bullet points per slide, written in professional academic presentation style to ensure clarity and consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By inserting this two-stage QA step between planning and slide generation, we prevent the propagation of factual errors, ensure that all high-value content from the source paper is represented, and maintain both the completeness and reliability of the automated presentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Multi-modal Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding steps produce a structured slide plan in JSON format—either directly from the Planner Agent or, if necessary, after refinement through the Verification–Adjustment process. To transform this abstract plan into an actual multi-modal slide deck containing text, figures, tables, and equations, we generate <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source code and compile it into PDF slides. We adopt <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Beamer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">(</span><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tantau et al., 2024</a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">)</span></cite></span> as the presentation framework due to its precise typography, native mathematical notation support, and flexible layout control, which make it particularly suitable for academic contexts. The <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generator Agent</span> parses the JSON blueprint and, using a user-supplied <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Beamer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">(</span><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tantau et al., 2024</a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">)</span></cite></span> theme (where the theme specifies the color scheme and all other style parameters follow its defaults), calls an LLM to produce <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> code for each slide: converting textual content into bullet points or paragraph blocks, embedding and positioning figures according to the theme’s layout rules, reconstructing tables—often provided as Markdown—into <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tabular</span> environments, and preserving any mathematical expressions in their original form. The generated <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source is compiled to PDF, and if compilation fails, an automatic correction routine captures the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.log</span> file and the faulty source, passes them to the LLM for targeted fixes, and re-compiles until successful or a preset attempt limit is reached. The final output is a complete, visually consistent academic presentation faithfully implementing the verified slide plan.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11062v1/section/img/interactive_pre.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interactive optimization workflow.</span> The user issues a natural language request to modify the presentation—for example, adding an explanatory slide on a specific concept. The <span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Editor Agent</span> interprets the request, locates the relevant slide segments, performs content retrieval and code-level modifications using a ReAct<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>-style process, compiles the updated LaTeX source, and returns the revised slide deck to the user.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Interactive Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the automated pipeline produces accurate and concise slides, audience backgrounds and preferences can vary widely, making it difficult for a single static deck to satisfy every use case. To enable on-demand refinement, we introduce the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Editor Agent</span>, which accepts natural language instructions and modifies the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source accordingly. The agent operates in a ReAct<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>-style loop: in the reasoning stage, the user request is decomposed into a sequence of actions. Each action is represented by an <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">action</span> type and an accompanying natural-language <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">description</span> that specifies, in plain words, what that step should accomplish. This description becomes the input to the corresponding tool, and—since most tools require generating or transforming natural-language and code—all of them call an LLM during execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our implementation, the Editor Agent can invoke the following operations:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">locate</span>: Identify the target region in the slide source code for editing. The description guides the LLM in matching either an explicitly numbered slide (<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\frame</span> block) or semantically locating the most relevant code segment from the structured slide plan and <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span>: Retrieve additional background content when the request is to add material not present in the current slides. Using the retrieval keywords embedded in the description, the tool scans the original paper’s references (via keyword matching and regex parsing) to find the most relevant citations. Citation metadata is used to query external APIs (arXiv, Semantic Scholar) for the full text of the cited papers. The Marker<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DataLab, 2024</a>)</cite> model is applied to parse these texts, and the extracted relevant sections—guided by the insertion context from <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">locate</span>—are integrated by the LLM into a slide-ready <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> snippet.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">modify</span>: Guided by the description, the LLM rewrites the code block returned by <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">locate</span> to incorporate the requested changes, then replaces the original block in the <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">insert</span>: Guided by the description, the LLM produces new slides or content blocks to be placed at the specified position without overwriting existing material. The content can be based directly on the user’s request or on results from <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">delete</span>: Guided by the description, the LLM confirms the targeted code block and removes it by replacing it with an empty string, thus deleting the corresponding slide content.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Multi-modal Generation ‣ 3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates such an interaction: the user requests to <em id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“add one page to explain attention in the method part,”</em> the agent’s reasoning produces a plan with a <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> step (keyword “attention”), a <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">locate</span> step targeting specific frames, and an <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">insert</span> step to add the generated explanation. The updated <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> source is then compiled into a revised slide deck and returned to the user.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a comprehensive evaluation of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span>, covering the system’s overall effectiveness, the contribution of key functional modules, and user experience in realistic academic communication and learning scenarios.
We assess four aspects:
(i) the effectiveness of interactive editing functions on learner comprehension, sense of control, and engagement (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>);
(ii) a comparison between slide<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>based learning and LLM chat<span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based learning (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. User Study 2: Comparing Slides and LLM Chat-based Learning from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>);
(iii) the impact of narrative structure optimization on the perceived quality of slides (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>); and
(iv) the effect of the enhanced Parser Agent and Verification–Adjustment Agents on the fidelity of structured information and factual accuracy (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by these research aims, we adopted a multi-method evaluation strategy to assess the effectiveness and usability of our system.
To this end, we designed three user studies (Sections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>) and one automated LLM judging–based ablation evaluation (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>), combining qualitative and quantitative analyses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure comparability and reproducibility, the following settings were applied across experiments unless otherwise noted.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">System Variants.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the full <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> system (Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we constructed several ablation variants as baselines:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o InfoExt</span> – removing the second<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>stage parsing in the Parser Agent;
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o VerifLoop</span> – removing the Verification–Adjustment Agents;
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o NarrOpt</span> – omitting the narrative structure optimization in the Planner Agent, used as a baseline in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and Materials.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the automated evaluation (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>), we used a collection of academic papers from diverse domains as input to generate slides. For comparability, the generated <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> slides were uniformly converted into a structured <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">slides.json</span> format (containing slide numbers and plain text without formatting) before being presented for LLM judgment.
In the user studies (Sections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), participants viewed the generated slides in PDF format with full layout and figures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models and Tooling.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All LLM-based components in <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> were implemented using <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> (OpenAI), as described in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with standardized API settings (temperature = 0.2).
In automated evaluations (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>), we used <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> (temperature = 0) to compute the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">table fidelity</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">content accuracy</span> metrics via bidirectional pairwise judgments, and employed <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>3.7<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Sonnet<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> (temperature = 0) to independently evaluate table fidelity for metric robustness analysis.
Prompt templates and detailed evaluation setups are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4" title="Generic System Prompt Template ‣ A.5. LLM-as-Judge Evaluation Materials ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation and Statistical Analysis.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Likert<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>scale questionnaire scores for each evaluation dimension were averaged across their constituent items (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2. Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Slides vs. LLM Chat-based Learning ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>, <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Expert Evaluation of Narrative Structure Optimization ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>),
providing composite measures of participants’ subjective perceptions on targeted usability and learning<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>related dimensions.
Depending on study design, two<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>tailed one<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>sample or paired<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>sample <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>tests were performed to examine whether mean scores differed significantly from a neutral baseline or between conditions (significance threshold <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>), thereby assessing the presence of meaningful performance differences.
Where multiple dimensions were analysed within the same study, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>values were adjusted using the Holm–Bonferroni method<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holm, 1979</a>)</cite> to control the familywise error rate.
Effect sizes (Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>) were calculated to quantify the magnitude and practical significance of observed effects beyond statistical reliability, interpreted following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakens (2013)</a></cite>.
For automated evaluation, system outputs were compared using win/tie/loss rates to indicate relative quality preferences, and forward–reverse agreement was computed to assess the stability of LLM<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text">-</span>based judgments with respect to position effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additional technical details can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal.</span> Beyond static slide generation, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> enables learners to interactively adapt content to their own learning needs.
This study explores how such customization capability affects learners’ subjective perceptions of comprehension, engagement, and control when studying a research paper outside their primary domain.
Our focus here is on typical audience members rather than content creators, reflecting the real-world scenario of conference attendees or students learning from research presentations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Participants.</span>
We recruited 30 undergraduate students (18–22 years old) from diverse majors, including computer science, mathematics, physics, chemistry, materials science, and biology.
All reported prior experience reading academic papers but had not been involved in the development of our system or study materials.
Participants were recruited via campus mailing lists, with a roughly balanced gender distribution (16 female, 14 male), and all were proficient in English.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Materials.</span>
The learning material was drawn from a recently published, award<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>winning HCI research paper<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cucumak and Subasi, 2025</a>)</cite> on urban community–animal interactions, chosen for its strong narrative structure and minimal reliance on domain<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>specific technical knowledge, in order to reduce comprehension barriers across different academic backgrounds.
An initial slide deck was automatically generated from the paper using the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> workflow described in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, providing concise textual summaries alongside key figures and diagrams.
We also enabled the interactive editing and augmentation functions in the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> interface, allowing participants to add, remove, or elaborate on slide content according to their learning needs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure.</span>
Participants first read through the automatically generated initial slide deck at their own pace.
They then used the interactive slide editing functions to modify and expand the deck until they felt they had sufficiently understood the paper’s content.
No strict time limit was imposed, but most sessions lasted between 15 and 25 minutes in total.
Interaction logs (<em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, number of edits, added elements) were recorded for exploratory analysis, though the primary focus of this study was participants’ subjective perceptions.
Immediately after completing the interactive learning session, participants filled out a 7-point Likert-scale questionnaire (1 = Strongly Disagree, 7 = Strongly Agree) assessing two key dimensions: <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learning Enhancement</span> (<em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“The interactive functions made it easier for me to understand the paper”</em>) and <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Control and Agency</span> (<em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“I felt in control of how I viewed and organized the paper’s content”</em>).
Each dimension was measured by multiple items (full wording in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2. Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>); internal consistency was acceptable for both scales (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;.80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>.80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;.80</annotation></semantics></math>).
We conceptualized this study as an exploratory single-condition evaluation aimed at gauging subjective acceptance and perceived utility, without a non-interactive control condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We measured learners’ perceptions of the interactive slide functions along two dimensions:
<em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning Enhancement</em> (perceived support for understanding new or difficult concepts, integration with paper logic) and
<em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Control and Agency</em> (perceived ability to customize, self<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>pace, and direct one’s learning).
Each dimension was assessed with four 7<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>point Likert items (1 = Strongly Disagree, 7 = Strongly Agree), averaged to form composite scores.
The complete questionnaire items are listed in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2. Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Results of <span id="S4.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Study 1</span> (Learners’ perspectives on interactive slide functions).
One-sample <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T1.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>tests compared mean ratings against the neutral midpoint of 4 (two-tailed, uncorrected).
Higher scores indicate more positive perceptions.
<span id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion:</span> Learners reported significantly above<span id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_text">-</span>neutral ratings for both <em id="S4.T1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning Enhancement</em> and <em id="S4.T1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Control and Agency</em>, indicating perceived benefits in comprehension and autonomy.</figcaption>
