</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Participants responded positively to the interactive slide functions, with mean ratings on both <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning Enhancement</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Control and Agency</em> well above the neutral midpoint (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
This indicates that learners generally perceived the interactive features as effective in strengthening understanding and giving them autonomy over their learning process.
Qualitative feedback further supported these findings: many participants described the functions as helping them <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“focus on key points”</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“organize information in my own way,”</em> while a few cautioned that fine<span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuning slides could sometimes distract from absorbing content.
Overall, results suggest that the interactive revision mechanism was both engaging and perceived as pedagogically beneficial.
This pattern may be attributable to the ability to reorganise and selectively adjust content, which gave learners a sense of ownership and active participation, potentially reinforcing engagement and deeper processing of the material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>User Study 2: Comparing Slides and LLM Chat-based Learning from Learners’ Perspective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal.</span> To contextualize the role of automatically generated slides among AI-assisted learning tools, this study directly compares slide-based learning with a LLM chat interface for reading academic papers.
We aimed to identify relative strengths and weaknesses across multiple aspects such as clarity, structural organization, learning support, engagement, and overall user preference, while also uncovering opportunities for hybrid workflows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Participants.</span>
We recruited 24 researchers (12 female, 12 male; mean age = 22.8, SD = 4.2) with academic backgrounds in HCI, computer science, or information science.
To capture varied LLM proficiency, half of the participants (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math>) reported using LLM chat tools (<em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, ChatGPT) for paper learning at least twice per month over the past three months, while the other half reported infrequent or no prior use.
All participants were fluent in English and had not read any of the target papers before the study.
They provided informed consent and received appropriate compensation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Materials.</span>
We prepared three peer-reviewed HCI research papers covering diverse topics and structures (<em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, empirical study, system design, theoretical framing).
All papers were unfamiliar to the participants (confirmed via screening).
Using our <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span> system, we automatically generated slide decks for each paper, summarizing motivation, methods, results, and conclusions with structured bullet points, key figures, and diagrams.
For the LLM chat condition, we used GPT-4<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023</a>)</cite> via ChatGPT, seeded with a standardized prompt:
<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Summarize the attached paper for efficient learning including key points, main findings, and relevant figures. Answer follow-up questions from the user.”</span>
The model was provided with the full paper in PDF form, and conversation history contained no extraneous context.
No manual post-editing was performed in either condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure</span>
We adopted a counterbalanced within<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>subject design: each participant experienced both learning conditions (slide<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based and LLM chat<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>based), each paired with a different paper.
The presentation order and paper–condition pairing were arranged so that all possible combinations occurred equally often across participants, thereby mitigating order and content effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each condition, participants were given up to 15 minutes to study the assigned paper using the given tool, with the option to finish earlier if they felt they had completed their learning.
In the Slide condition, participants freely navigated the automatically generated slides.
In the Chat condition, participants interacted with an LLM via a standardized web interface, starting with a fixed seed prompt and then asking any follow-up questions they wanted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To capture both immediate impressions and direct comparisons, we used a two-stage questionnaire approach:
1. <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Condition-specific absolute ratings:</span> Immediately after completing each condition, participants filled out a short 7-point Likert-scale questionnaire rating that method on five relevant aspects (<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Visual clarity</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Structural clarity</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Understanding and memory support</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learning flow and engagement</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall satisfaction</span>), without referencing the alternative method. This ensured that fresh impressions of each condition were recorded without memory decay or recency bias.
2. <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cross-condition comparative ratings:</span> After completing both conditions, participants filled out the comparison-focused questionnaire shown in Table <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Evaluation Rubric. ‣ A.3. Slides vs. LLM Chat-based Learning ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, in which each item directly contrasted the slide-based format with the LLM chat format (7-point Likert scale: 1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree). These items measured the same five evaluation dimensions as the absolute ratings, each with two comparison statements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, participants completed a brief post-study survey with a single forced-choice question (<span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Which learning method do you prefer overall?</span>) and two open-ended prompts inviting them to explain their choice and suggest possible hybrid workflows combining the two methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We compared participants’ experiences with the slide<span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>based format and their typical LLM chat<span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based learning across five dimensions:
<em id="S4.SS3.p8.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Visual Clarity &amp; Intuitiveness</em>, <em id="S4.SS3.p8.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Structural Clarity &amp; Organization</em>, <em id="S4.SS3.p8.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning Flow &amp; Engagement</em>, <em id="S4.SS3.p8.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Support for Understanding &amp; Memory</em>, and
<em id="S4.SS3.p8.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Overall Satisfaction &amp; Future Preference</em>.
Each dimension comprised two 7<span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>point Likert items (1 = Strongly Disagree, 7 = Strongly Agree), averaged to obtain composite scores.
Dimension definitions focused respectively on visual presentation quality, informational structure, perceived engagement, cognitive support, and overall preference.
Full wording of items is given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Slides vs. LLM Chat-based Learning ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Results of <span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Study 2</span> (Absolute ratings per condition: Slides <em id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs.</em> LLM Chat).
Paired-sample <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text">-</span>tests with Holm–Bonferroni correction; <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> = Cohen’s <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for paired samples.
Ratings are on a 7-point Likert scale (higher = better).
<span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion:</span> The slide-based format was rated significantly higher than LLM chat for visual clarity, structural clarity, and (to a lesser extent) support for understanding/memory, while engagement and overall satisfaction were similar.</figcaption>
