<td id="S4.T2.10.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.10 (0.68)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.92</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;.05^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>.05</mn><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;.05^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.60</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.10.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Learning flow &amp; engagement</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.15 (0.70)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.05 (0.75)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.51</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.616</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.10</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.10.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Overall satisfaction</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">5.25 (0.68)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">5.10 (0.73)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.02</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">.318</td>
<td id="S4.T2.10.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.21</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Across the clarity-related metrics, the slide<span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>based format was consistently rated higher than participants’ typical LLM chat<span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based learning, while measures of engagement and satisfaction were comparable (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3. User Study 2: Comparing Slides and LLM Chat-based Learning from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Slides were viewed as visually clearer, more structurally organized, and somewhat stronger in supporting understanding and memory.
No reliable differences emerged for learning flow or overall enjoyment, suggesting both formats were similarly engaging.
The comparative questionnaire administered after both conditions (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Slides vs. LLM Chat-based Learning ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) confirmed these trends: slides were judged superior in visual and structural clarity, moderately better in aiding comprehension, and only marginally better for engagement and satisfaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">When asked for an overall preference, most participants chose the slide format, a smaller number preferred LLM chat, and a few expressed no strong preference.
Open<span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>ended comments revealed three recurring themes:
(1) <span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Visual and structural grounding</span> — slides made the paper’s main points easier to grasp at a glance;
(2) <span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Interactive exploration</span> — LLM chat offered personalised question<span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>answering and opportunistic deep dives;
(3) <span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hybrid workflow potential</span> — many envisioned starting with slides for an overview, then turning to LLM chat for follow<span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>up queries.
Taken together, these findings suggest that slides provide a stronger structural scaffold, while chat supports personalised exploration, indicating a complementary relationship.
Such complementarity offers valuable implications for future educational tools, as learners relying solely on open<span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>ended LLM chat may not always know what questions to ask, whereas slides can provide a prepared, structured foundation to ensure that essential content is covered before exploration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal.</span> This study investigates whether reorganizing extracted paper content from the conventional IMRaD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Day and Gastel, 2012</a>)</cite> structure into a Problem–Motivation–Results–Conclusion (PMRC) narrative improves the quality of automatically generated slides.
By enlisting domain experts highly familiar with the source papers, we aimed to obtain informed, fine-grained judgments on content accuracy, information density, and narrative flow, comparing the full system against an ablated version lacking the narrative structuring step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Participants.</span>
We recruited eight experienced computer science researchers, comprising three faculty PIs, three Ph.D. candidates, and two research assistants. All participants had extensive experience in academic paper writing and conference presentations, and were therefore well qualified to judge the accuracy and communicative quality of generated slides.
The sample included five male and three female experts, aged between 25 and 46 years (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=33.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=33.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=6.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=6.8</annotation></semantics></math>).
Participants were asked to bring two papers they had authored or were highly familiar with, ensuring they could reliably assess content accuracy.
The study was approved by our institutional ethics board, and all participants provided informed consent. Each received a small honorarium for their time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Materials.</span>
For each of the two expert-supplied papers, we generated a slide deck using one of two configurations of our <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slide</span> system:
(1) <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With narrative structure optimization</span> — the full pipeline as described in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, in which the <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Planner Agent</span> reorganizes the parsed paper content from its original IMRaD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Day and Gastel, 2012</a>)</cite> sequence into a Problem–Motivation–Results–Conclusion (PMRC) presentation narrative, guided by cognitive principles such as Cognitive Load Theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sweller, 1988</a>)</cite>, Mayer’s Multimedia Learning Principles<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer, 2009</a>)</cite>, and Dual Coding Theory<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paivio, 1971</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paivio, 1986</a>)</cite>.
(2) <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Without narrative structure optimization</span> — an ablation variant in which the <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Planner Agent</span> preserves the original paper’s IMRaD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Day and Gastel, 2012</a>)</cite> order when allocating content to slides, omitting the PMRC re<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>sequencing step and associated refinements, while all other pipeline stages (high<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text">-</span>fidelity parsing, quality verification, LaTeX slide generation) remained identical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Both decks were generated automatically without human post<span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>editing.
The evaluation rubric (Table <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Evaluation Rubric. ‣ A.4. Expert Evaluation of Narrative Structure Optimization ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) comprised three dimensions — <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information Density</span>, and <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Narrative Structure &amp; Flow</span> — each assessed via three 7<span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>point Likert items (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure.</span>
For each participant, one of their papers was randomly assigned to the “with optimization” configuration and the other to the “without optimization” configuration, with this assignment counterbalanced across participants to control for paper<span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>specific effects.
The order in which the two slide decks were presented was randomized to mitigate order effects.
Participants reviewed each deck in turn and then completed the 9<span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>item rubric for that deck. Ratings were entered directly into a digital form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
Experts evaluated the generated slide decks (with and without narrative optimization) along three dimensions:
<em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em> (factual correctness of statements, terminology, and reported results),
<em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Information Density</em> (appropriateness of detail level and balance of content per slide), and
<em id="S4.SS4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Narrative Structure &amp; Flow</em> (logical progression and smoothness of transitions).
Each dimension was scored using three 7<span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>point Likert items (1 = Strongly Disagree, 7 = Strongly Agree), averaged into composite scores for analysis.
The full rubric is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Expert Evaluation of Narrative Structure Optimization ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Results of <span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Study 3</span> (Expert evaluation of slides with <em id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs.</em> without narrative structure optimization, <math id="S4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>).
Ratings are mean scores across three items per dimension (7-point Likert scale). <math id="S4.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> = Cohen’s <math id="S4.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for paired samples.
<span id="S4.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion:</span> Narrative structure optimization significantly improved experts’ ratings of content accuracy and narrative flow, without altering perceived information density.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dimension</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">With Opt M (SD)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.8.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Without Opt M (SD)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T3.8.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(7)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T3.8.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
