<td id="S4.T3.8.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.07</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Slides produced with narrative structure optimization were rated higher than their ablation counterparts in both <em id="S4.SS4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em> and <em id="S4.SS4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Narrative Structure &amp; Flow</em>, with no notable change in <em id="S4.SS4.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Information Density</em> (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
This suggests that the added structuring improved perceived organisation and flow without making slides feel either too sparse or overloaded.
Experts also noted that navigation through concepts felt smoother and that key findings were presented more faithfully.
The absence of differences in information density indicates that narrative enhancements did not affect the perceived amount of content, but instead influenced how that content was organised and understood.
Overall, these results support that narrative optimization can meaningfully enhance both clarity and accuracy in automatically generated academic slides.
For audiences, a clear and well<span id="S4.SS4.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>paced narrative flow can lower cognitive load, making it easier to follow complex arguments and verify key evidence, which is especially valuable in dense academic presentations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal.</span> This experiment evaluates two key components in <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span>: (i) whether the enhanced information extraction in the Parser Agent improves the fidelity of complex tables and equations; and (ii) whether the Verification–Adjustment Agents further enhance factual accuracy beyond the base pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup and Baselines.</span> We selected 14 academic papers from diverse domains and generated slides using three system variants:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full</span>: the complete <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> pipeline (Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o InfoExt</span>: removing the second<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>stage parsing of the Parser Agent (Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), used as the baseline for the <em id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em> comparison.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o VerifLoop</span>: removing the Verification–Adjustment Agents (Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Method ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), used as the baseline for the <em id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em> comparison.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For consistency across variants, the <span id="S4.SS5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S4.SS5.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S4.SS5.p2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> slides produced by each pipeline were parsed into a structured <span id="S4.SS5.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">slides.json</span> format containing slide numbers and plain text (formatting removed) to eliminate layout differences when judged. In each ablation scenario, paired slide decks from the Full system and the corresponding ablation system were compared by LLM judges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Protocol and Metrics.</span> Slide pairs were anonymized and presented to the judge model without revealing system identity. The model applied a five<span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>point comparative scale (<em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">A significantly better</em>, <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">A slightly better</em>, <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tie</em>, <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">B slightly better</em>, <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">B significantly better</em>), which was then collapsed into win / tie / loss for aggregation. To control for position bias, each pair was evaluated in both <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">forward</span> (Full<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Ablation) and <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reverse</span> (Ablation<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Full) orders. The <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward–reverse agreement rate</em> (FRA) was recorded to quantify position sensitivity, and bidirectional results were aggregated at the pair level.</p>
</div>
