<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The primary judge model was <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>, which was used for both evaluation metrics. For <em id="S4.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em>, we additionally used <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>3.7<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Sonnet<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> to replicate the evaluation. Two evaluation metrics (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) were used:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em>: preservation of tables, mathematical formulas, and other structured data, considering formatting, structure, numerical precision, and technical details.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em>: factual correctness, logical consistency, and completeness of methodology and conclusions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the reliability of the LLM-as-judge protocol, we conducted a small human evaluation on a random subset of 5 papers from the <em id="S4.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em> condition. Two independent human raters (Ph.D. students in computer science, not involved in system development) compared paired slide decks using the same rubric and blinded protocol. The majority opinion agreed with GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>’s win/tie/loss decision in 9 of 10 comparisons (90% agreement), with inter<span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>rater agreement <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.82</annotation></semantics></math>. This alignment suggests that the LLM-based evaluations are consistent with domain-informed human judgements. Detailed protocol and results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6.SSS0.Px7" title="Human Validation of LLM-as-Judge. ‣ A.6. LLM-as-Judge Evaluation Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>, and full prompt templates and judging materials in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4" title="Generic System Prompt Template ‣ A.5. LLM-as-Judge Evaluation Materials ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Enhanced parsing yielded substantial gains in table and formula fidelity, confirmed by two judge models; verification–adjustment mainly improved robustness in borderline cases.
Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarises aggregate bidirectional win rates, tie rates, and forward–reverse agreement for each metric and judge.
For <em id="S4.SS5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em>, GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> judged the Full system superior in 67.9% of comparisons (<em id="S4.SS5.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs.</em> 17.9% losses), with a tie rate of 14.3% and an FRA of 78.6%. Claude produced an identical win rate and tie rate, but with a lower FRA (50.0%), indicating higher position sensitivity while confirming the same directional advantage. This shows that the enhanced Parser Agent is critical for preserving complex tables and formulas.
For <em id="S4.SS5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em>, GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> results were dominated by ties (85.7%), with a 7.1% win rate for Full and no cases where the ablation prevailed. The base pipeline already performs strongly in preserving factual accuracy, and the Verification–Adjustment Agents mainly improve robustness in certain borderline cases, without degrading output.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Results of the <span id="S4.T5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-judge</span> bidirectional ablation evaluation (Full system <em id="S4.T5.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs.</em> ablated variants).
Win/tie rates are aggregated across both forward and reverse presentation orders;
<em id="S4.T5.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Agreement</em> = proportion of paper pairs with consistent forward–reverse judgments.
<span id="S4.T5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion:</span> The enhanced Parser Agent (<em id="S4.T5.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Table Fidelity</em> metric) was judged superior in over two<span id="S4.T5.15" class="ltx_text">-</span>thirds of comparisons by both GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> and Claude<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a>)</cite>, while the Verification–Adjustment Agents yielded smaller, tie<span id="S4.T5.16" class="ltx_text">-</span>dominated gains in <em id="S4.T5.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Content Accuracy</em> without degrading results.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.18" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.18.1" class="ltx_tr">
