<th id="S4.T5.18.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.18.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.18.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.18.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Win</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.18.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.18.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tie</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.18.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.18.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agreement</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.18.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.18.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T5.18.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-6.5pt;">Table Fidelity</span></th>
<td id="S4.T5.18.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">GPT-4o</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">67.9% (19/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">14.3% (4/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">78.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.18.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.18.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 3.0pt;"></th>
<td id="S4.T5.18.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 3.0pt;">Claude</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 3.0pt;">67.9% (19/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 3.0pt;">14.3% (4/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 3.0pt;">50.0%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.18.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.18.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Content Accuracy</th>
<td id="S4.T5.18.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">GPT-4o</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">7.1% (2/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">85.7% (24/28)</td>
<td id="S4.T5.18.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">71.4%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary.</span> Enhanced parsing consistently improves multi<span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>modal content fidelity, and verification–adjustment strengthens reliability in selective scenarios. Bidirectional comparisons and replication across judge models help control for bias and increase confidence in these findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, high-fidelity parsing was critical for preserving structured multimodal content, and verification–adjustment strengthened factual robustness without harm. Cross-model replication and bidirectional agreement bolster confidence in these conclusions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> already covers a broad range of academic paper formats and static multimodal elements, certain content types and usage scenarios remain outside the current scope, providing natural directions for future development.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the present system focuses on static modalities—text, figures, tables, and mathematical expressions—extracted from PDF papers. It does not yet integrate dynamic or interactive media such as embedded videos, animations, interactive plots, or executable code blocks that are increasingly common in modern publications. Extending the parsing and generation pipeline to handle such media would enable richer, more engaging slide decks that better capture the full communicative intent of the original work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, many research papers include extensive supplementary materials, such as large appendices, datasets, code repositories, or domain<span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>specific multimedia attachments. These are currently not incorporated into the automated slide generation process. Future work could explore selective inclusion of supplementary resources, either by summarizing their key contributions or by adding optional “deep dive” slides tailored to a user’s interests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the current interactive editor relies on natural<span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>language commands to modify LaTeX slide code. While powerful, this may feel indirect for some users who prefer to see and adjust slides directly. Future work could provide a visual editing interface similar to PowerPoint, where users drag, type, or rearrange elements and immediately see the final layout update on screen. This would make refinement more intuitive for users with no LaTeX background, while keeping the system’s intelligent automation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By extending <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S5.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> to support a wider range of media formats, supplementary resources, and editing interfaces, we aim to broaden its applicability and enhance its role as a comprehensive AI assistant for academic learning and presentation preparation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> , a LLM<span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>driven multi<span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>agent system that automatically converts academic papers into structured, visually enriched, and pedagogically optimized slide decks. By combining high<span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>fidelity multimodal parsing, cognitive<span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>science<span id="S6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>guided narrative restructuring, a verification–adjustment loop for accuracy, and an interactive natural<span id="S6.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>language editor for personalized refinement, <span id="S6.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto<span id="S6.p1.1.8.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Slides</span> bridges the gap between scholarly writing and effective teaching materials. User studies and automated evaluations show that our approach improves comprehension, retention, and engagement while preserving the fidelity of complex figures, tables, and formulas. This work advances AI<span id="S6.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>assisted academic communication, making complex research more accessible and learnable, and laying the groundwork for richer, more engaging scholarly dissemination tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
