<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1. </span>Appendix Overview</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides supplementary materials and complete experimental details referenced in the main text.
It follows the order of the experiments presented in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learner Study on interactive slide functions (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparative Study: Slide-based vs LLM Chat-based learning (§<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. User Study 2: Comparing Slides and LLM Chat-based Learning from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expert Evaluation of narrative structure optimization (§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. User Study 3: Evaluating Narrative Structure Optimization from Experts’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-as-Judge component ablation (§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Automatic Evaluation: LLM-as-Judge Component Ablation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each study, we provide the full evaluation rubrics, raw results, additional descriptive and inferential statistics, and any figures/tables omitted from the main paper for brevity. For the LLM-as-Judge evaluation, we also list prompt templates, preprocessing details, full per-paper results, and a human validation protocol to ensure the reliability of LLM-based judgments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2. </span>Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides the complete evaluation rubric and quantitative results for User Study 1 (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), which examined learners’ perceived comprehension support and control when using the interactive editing features of <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auto-Slides</span>.
The rubric covers two composite evaluation dimensions: <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Learning Enhancement</em> and <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Control and Agency</em>, each measured by multiple 7-point Likert items.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Rubric.</span>
The full set of items included in the post-task questionnaire is given in Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.2. Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, allowing replication in future work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6. </span>Evaluation rubric for learner study (7-point Likert scale: 1=Strongly Disagree, 7=Strongly Agree). Completed after using the interactive revision function.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T6.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T6.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T6.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T6.4.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation Dimension</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A1.T6.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:258.8pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Specific Assessment Items</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T6.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T6.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T6.4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Learning Enhancement</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T6.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:258.8pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive function helps me better understand concepts I was previously unfamiliar with</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive revision helps me grasp the methodology or key steps of the paper more clearly</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive function helps me clarify previously confusing parts of the paper</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive revision helps me connect supplemental content with the overall logic of the paper</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T6.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T6.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T6.4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Control and Agency</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T6.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.9pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:258.8pt;">
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive function allows me to customize learning content according to my needs</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive function helps me actively identify and resolve parts I do not understand</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•The interactive function lets me explore content at my own pace</span></span>
<span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T6.4.3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">•Using the interactive revision gives me a sense of control over my learning process</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Detailed Results.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Detailed Results. ‣ A.2. Evaluating the Impact of Interactive Slide Functions on Learner Experience ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> reports detailed descriptive and inferential statistics for each evaluation dimension, supplementing the summary in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2. User Study 1: Interactive Slide Functions from Learners’ Perspective ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Auto-Slides: An Interactive Multi-Agent System for Creating and Customizing Research Presentations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> of the main text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7. </span>Detailed results for the interactive slide function study (<math id="A1.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=30</annotation></semantics></math>). Ratings are on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree). <math id="A1.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> = Cohen’s <math id="A1.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for one-sample test <em id="A1.T7.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vs.</em> 4.0.</figcaption>
